Let the host point the assembler at a staged 'tileiras'.

assembleWithExecutable() locates the Tile IR assembler with
llvm::sys::findProgramByName, which searches PATH and nothing else, and runs
it in the environment of the calling process. A build system that stages its
tools in a private directory -- bazel puts them in the test or binary
runfiles -- has no way to be found that way short of mutating the process
environment. Without it the compiler silently falls back to emitting Tile IR
bytecode and leaves the assembly to the driver's JIT.

Add setTileIRAssembler(path, environment). The PATH lookup and the inherited
environment stay the default; an empty path or environment restores each.
The environment lets a host that stages the tool also tell it where its
companions live (tileiras reads CUDA_HOME for libnvvm and ptxas) without
touching the host's own environment.
